  "2016 P2 Q5": {"v":"angles.two-tangents-chord","marks":[1,1,1],"shows":["\\(37\\)","\\(53\\)",null],"marksLine":"3 marks","nudge":"What do you know about the angle between a tangent and a radius? What do the parallel lines tell you about angle OAC?","step":"ED and AC are parallel, so angles AOD and OAC add to \\(180^\\circ\\). Use that to find angle OAC.","watch":"The tangent meets the radius OA at \\(90^\\circ\\). Tangents from B are equal in length, so triangle ABC is isosceles."},
  "2016 P2 Q6": {"v":"data.mean-sd-compare-2016","marks":[2,2,1,1],"shows":["\\(13\\) (minutes)","\\(\\sqrt{\\frac{164}{5}}\\)",null,null],"marksLine":"6 marks","nudge":"In (a), which formula on the formula list gives the standard deviation? In (b), what do the mean and the standard deviation each tell you?","step":"Add the six times and divide by \\(6\\) for the mean. Then take the mean from each time and square each difference.","watch":"In (b), one comment on the mean (the average waiting time) and one on the standard deviation (how spread out the times are), naming Jack or Sophie."},
  "2016 P2 Q7": {"v":"volume.cone-minus-cone","marks":[1,1,1,2],"shows":["difference in two volumes","\\(\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\pi \\times 16^2 \\times 24 (= 6433.98...)\\)","\\(\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\pi \\times 9^2 \\times 13.5 (= 1145.11...)\\)","\\(5288.87...\\)"],"marksLine":"5 marks","nudge":"The carton is a large cone with a small cone taken off. Which two volumes do you need?","step":"Work out the volume of the large cone with \\(V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\\), using half of its diameter.","watch":"Use each cone's own radius and height, and take the small cone away. Round to 2 significant figures only at the end."},
  "2016 P2 Q8": {"v":"trig-diagram.sine-angle-pre2023","marks":[1,1,1],"shows":["\\(\\frac{\\sin x}{150} = \\frac{\\sin 66}{140}\\)","\\(\\sin x = \\frac{150 \\sin 66}{140}\\)",null],"marksLine":"3 marks","nudge":"You know two sides and one angle, not between them. Which rule links sides with the angles opposite them?","step":"Pair each leg with the angle facing it: the 140 cm leg faces the \\(66^\\circ\\) angle, and the 150 cm leg faces \\(x^\\circ\\).","watch":"Put \\(\\sin x^\\circ\\) on top in the sine rule, then take the inverse sine at the end."},
