  "2025 P1 Q15": {"v":"form-equation.rectangle-square","marks":[1,2,2,1],"shows":["\\((2x + 3)(x + 1)\\)","\\(2x^2 + 2x + 3x + 3 = (x + 3)^2\\) OR \\((2x + 3)(x + 1) = x^2 + 3x + 3x + 9\\)","\\((x - 3)(x + 2)\\)",null],"marksLine":"6 marks","nudge":"How do you find a rectangle's area from its length and breadth? What is the area of the square?","step":"For (a), multiply the length of the rectangle by its breadth, and leave it as a pair of brackets.","watch":"In (c) a length cannot be negative, so reject one root, then put the other back into both sides to get the length and the breadth."},
  "2025 P2 Q1": {"v":"percentages.compound-between-years","marks":[1,1,1],"shows":["\\(\\times 1.04\\)","\\(118750 \\times 1.04^2\\)",null],"marksLine":"3 marks","nudge":"An increase of \\(4\\%\\) a year: what do you multiply by each year, and how many times?","step":"Write the \\(4\\%\\) increase as a decimal multiplier: the whole amount plus the extra.","watch":"Apply the multiplier twice, once for each year (a power of \\(2\\)), not \\(8\\%\\) in one go."},
  "2025 P2 Q2": {"v":"volume.sphere","marks":[1,1,1],"shows":["\\(\\frac{4}{3} \\times \\pi \\times 10.5^3\\)","\\(4849(.0...)\\)",null],"marksLine":"3 marks","nudge":"The volume formula for a sphere uses the radius. What is the radius here?","step":"Halve the 21 cm diameter to get the radius, then use \\(V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\).","watch":"Use the radius, not the diameter, and round to 3 significant figures only at the end."},
  "2025 P2 Q3": {"v":"sci-notation.calculate","marks":[1,1],"shows":["\\(\\frac{6.1}{100} \\times (3.27 \\times 10^{-22})\\)",null],"marksLine":"2 marks","nudge":"How do you find \\(6.1\\%\\) of an amount on a calculator?","step":"Write \\(6.1\\%\\) as a decimal and multiply it by the gold atom's mass.","watch":"Give the answer in scientific notation, with a number between \\(1\\) and \\(10\\) in front of the power of ten."},
  "2025 P2 Q4": {"v":"data.mean-sd-compare","marks":[2,2,1,1],"shows":[null,"\\(\\sqrt{\\frac{300}{6}}\\)","on average the weights of the rugby players in Scotland are lower.",null],"marksLine":"6 marks","nudge":"In (a), which formula on the formula list gives the standard deviation? In (b), what do the mean and the standard deviation each tell you?","step":"Add the seven weights and divide by \\(7\\) for the mean. Then take the mean from each weight and square each difference.","watch":"In (b), one comment on the mean and one on the standard deviation, each about the weights of the players in Scotland or France: not just \"the mean is lower\"."},
